# Omarchy-style navigation for macOS, the keybinding half. The window manager
# half is ../../yabai/.config/yabai/yabairc; together they do what
# aerospace.toml does on its own.
#
# Modifier map:  Super -> cmd,  Alt -> alt,  Ctrl -> ctrl,  Shift -> shift
# Every binding below mirrors https://omarchy.org/manual/hotkeys
#
# skhd grabs cmd-* globally, exactly as AeroSpace does, so the same
# compensation applies: the macOS menu commands it displaces (Open, Save, Print,
# Zoom, ...) are restored on plain ctrl by
# ../../aerospace/bin/macos-app-shortcuts.sh. Running it is not optional, and it
# is shared with the AeroSpace setup rather than copied -- it moves into a module
# of its own only if AeroSpace is deleted. `make macos-shortcuts`.
#
# NOTHING here binds cmd-f: Find stays native. See the fullscreen block below.
#
# Punctuation keys are written as kVK keycodes (0x1B minus, 0x18 equal, 0x32
# grave, 0x29 semicolon) rather than literals: the keycodes are stable, the
# literal keywords are not documented in skhd's grammar.
#
# yabai and jq are spelled out absolutely because skhd runs commands through
# $SHELL -c without a login shell, so /opt/homebrew/bin is not on PATH. skhd has
# no variables, so every path is written out in full.
#
# The workspace bindings need yabai's scripting addition on some macOS versions
# and not others -- see docs/yabai.md. `space --focus` and `window --space`
# stopped needing it in yabai 7.1.19 and 7.1.25, so cmd-1..9 and cmd-shift-1..9
# work regardless; sticky (cmd-o) does not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- modes
# Declared up front: skhd resolves a mode name when it parses the binding that
# uses it. `@` makes service mode capture every key, so a stray letter in there
# does nothing rather than reaching the app underneath.
:: default
:: service @

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- focus
# Super + Arrow = move focus, as in Omarchy. This takes the browser's Back /
# Forward (cmd-left / cmd-right); macos-app-shortcuts.sh puts those back on
# alt-left / alt-right, the Linux convention.
cmd - left  : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus west
cmd - down  : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus south
cmd - up    : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus north
cmd - right : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus east

# NB: no cmd-hjkl focus aliases -- Omarchy is arrow-based for focus, and
# cmd-j is its window-position toggle (see below).

# Super + Shift + Arrow = swap window
cmd + shift - left  : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap west
cmd + shift - down  : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap south
cmd + shift - up    : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap north
cmd + shift - right : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap east

# Alt + Tab = cycle windows   /   Ctrl + Alt + Tab = cycle monitors
# `|| --focus first` is AeroSpace's --wrap-around: yabai's next/prev fail at the
# end of the list rather than wrapping.
alt - tab                : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus next || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus first
alt + shift - tab        : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus prev || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus last
ctrl + alt - tab         : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m display --focus next || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m display --focus first
ctrl + alt + shift - tab : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m display --focus prev || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m display --focus last

